\section{Introduction}

Diversity is a fundamental yet challenging concept in evolutionary computation, proving essential for tackling complex problems. Despite its importance, current methods often yield results that are difficult to differentiate or produce redundant solutions. Moreover, existing approaches sometimes prioritize diversity at the expense of solution quality, or favor fitness at the cost of diversity, depending on the method used. These limitations create a critical need for new algorithms that can simultaneously achieve both goals. This paper examines multi-objective optimization, multimodal optimization, and a proposed novel approach named \textit{quality diversity}, which seeks to novel, high-performing solutions.

\paragraph{Related Work} Typically, diversity is measured by genotypic distance in the representation space \cite{BRSK15}, though some research focuses on the decision space, such as Pareto distance \cite{PDRK14}, modal distance \cite{ZhangEtal17}, or the extended Sorensen-Dice distance in quality diversity \cite{CluneEtal16a}. However, research rarely compares these methods empirically. Genotypic distances risk guiding the evolutionary process toward specific regions, acting as implicit objectives, yet no empirical evidence supports the impact of diverse solution types on final outcomes \cite{CluneEtal16a}. Additionally, no comprehensive empirical comparison exists among the three primary approaches: multi-objective optimization, multimodal optimization, and quality diversity.

